# Copy to .env for local development.
#
# Environment Configuration for Orebit Admin Backend
#
# Everything here is a secret or an infrastructure coordinate. Product policy —
# OTP lifetimes, attempt caps, cooldowns, password rules, TOTP window — lives in
# auth/auth.constants.ts instead, because a deploy should never be the mechanism
# for changing a cooldown.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3002
LOG_LEVEL=debug
ADMIN_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ─── PostgreSQL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The SAME instance the main Orebit backend uses. This service owns the `admin`
# Postgres schema; `identity` belongs to Orebit-backend and is not touched here.
#
# ?schema=admin IS REQUIRED AND LOAD-BEARING. Prisma writes its migration history
# to `_prisma_migrations` in the schema this parameter names, defaulting to
# `public`. Without it both services share one history table and each reads the
# other's migrations as unknown drift — the first `db:migrate` would appear to
# work and then fail confusingly on the second service. With it, this service
# keeps `admin._prisma_migrations` and the main backend keeps its own.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://orebit:orebit@localhost:5432/orebit_dev?schema=admin
DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://orebit:orebit@localhost:5432/orebit_dev?schema=admin

# The Prisma connection-pool ceiling, appended to DATABASE_URL as
# `connection_limit` at boot. It must be set here rather than left to Prisma,
# whose default is `num_physical_cpus * 2 + 1` read from the HOST — in a
# container that is the machine's core count, not this process's allowance, so
# the pool is sized for hardware it cannot use and every replica multiplies it
# against one Postgres `max_connections`.
#
# Do NOT also write `connection_limit` into DATABASE_URL: two authorities for one
# number, and the service refuses to boot if both are present.
#
# 10 is a starting point, not a tuned value. Size it from the production pool-wait
# metric and Postgres `max_connections` divided by the replica count.
PG_POOL_MAX=10

# ─── Redis ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rate-limit counters, session-revocation flags, WebAuthn challenges. Everything
# on this instance is reconstructible: docker-compose runs it with `--save ""
# --appendonly no --maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`, so it evicts freely under
# memory pressure and loses everything on restart. That is correct for a cache.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# The DURABLE instance — the outbound OTP queue, and nothing else.
#
# A SECOND INSTANCE, NOT A SECOND DATABASE, and the distinction is load-bearing.
# `maxmemory-policy` and AOF are server-wide settings, so two logical DBs on one
# instance would share the cache's eviction policy — and an evicted job is a
# sign-in code that never arrives. Since the emailed code is a mandatory second
# factor with no password-recovery flow behind it, that is an admin locked out by
# a cache eviction. A `FLUSHALL` aimed at the cache would take it too.
#
# docker-compose runs this one with `--appendonly yes --appendfsync everysec
# --maxmemory-policy noeviction` on port 6380.
REDIS_DURABLE_URL=redis://localhost:6380

# ─── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Argon2id `secret` parameter. 32 characters is a floor, not a style preference:
# a short value silently weakens every password hash.
#
# THE VALUE BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER AND IS PUBLISHED IN THIS REPOSITORY. Both
# src/config.ts (at boot, in production) and db/seed.ts (always) refuse it, so it
# cannot reach a real deployment by being forgotten.
#
# CANNOT BE ROTATED once an admin has been seeded. This is argon2's `secret`, not
# a salt — every stored hash is only verifiable with the exact value used to create
# it, so changing it later locks out every admin at once and the only remedy is
# re-seeding. Set the real value BEFORE the first `npm run db:seed`, and back it up
# with the same care as the database.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 36
PASSWORD_PEPPER=change-me-to-a-random-32-plus-character-secret

# ES256 (ECDSA P-256) JWT signing key — base64-encoded PKCS#8 PEM.
# One key signs both token types; what separates an access token from a refresh
# token is the pinned `aud` claim, not the key. Generate with:
#
#   openssl ecparam -name prime256v1 -genkey -noout \
#     | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt | base64 | tr -d '\n'
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=

# AES-256-GCM key for TOTP secrets — 32 raw bytes, base64.
#
# Unlike a password, a TOTP secret must be RECOVERABLE: verification recomputes
# HMAC-SHA1 from the shared secret, so it is encrypted rather than hashed. That
# makes this key unrotatable without re-enrolling every admin — losing it locks
# out every TOTP factor in the system. Back it up with the same care as the
# database. Generate with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
TOTP_SECRET_KEY=

# AES-256-GCM key for queued OTP job payloads — 32 raw bytes, base64.
#
# The queue is the one place in this service where a live one-time code is
# written to storage. Everywhere else a code exists only as a peppered digest:
# it is generated, emailed and forgotten inside a single request. A queued job
# has to carry the plaintext until a worker sends it, so this key is what keeps a
# dump of `redis-durable` — AOF files in a Docker volume, a far softer target
# than Postgres — from handing over every in-flight sign-in code.
#
# Its own key rather than reusing TOTP_SECRET_KEY, because the two have opposite
# lifetimes: a TOTP secret lives for years, a queued code for ninety seconds.
# Rotating this one is nearly free — the worst case is that jobs queued in the
# last ninety seconds fail to decrypt and their admins ask for a new code — so it
# should not be chained to a key that can never be rotated at all.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
OTP_QUEUE_KEY=

# AES-256-GCM key for the refresh token in a browser session record at rest.
# Required. Exactly 32 bytes, base64.
#
# The browser holds an opaque handle in `orebit_admin_session`; the backend holds
# the access and refresh tokens it names, in Redis. That puts a twelve-hour grant
# of admin access in the softer of the two stores this service writes to, so it is
# sealed. The access token in the same record is not — it lives fifteen minutes and
# is refused the moment its session is revoked.
#
# Its own key rather than reusing OTP_QUEUE_KEY or TOTP_SECRET_KEY, for the reason
# above: the three have different lifetimes and must be rotatable independently.
#
# Rotating this signs everyone out — every stored refresh token becomes
# unopenable, and admins sign in again. Nothing is lost beyond that.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
BFF_SESSION_KEY=

# ─── WebAuthn / passkeys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID must be the registrable domain, with no scheme and no port:
# `localhost` in development, `admin.orebit.io` in production. A passkey is bound
# to the RP ID it was registered under, so changing this invalidates every
# passkey already enrolled — treat it as permanent per environment.
#
# The expected origin is ADMIN_APP_URL, so that value must match the browser's
# address bar exactly, port included.
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Orebit Admin

# ─── Captcha ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ANY NUMBER of providers may be live at once — the same contract the consumer
# backend uses. A provider counts as live when its SECRET is set; the login page
# reads the live list from POST /auth/login {"step":"start"} and echoes back which
# one it used as `captchaProvider` on the password step.
#
# Site keys are public by design (they ship to the browser); secrets never leave
# here. Setting a secret without its site key fails to boot in production, because
# a live provider the browser cannot render is worse than one that is off.
#
# Leave every secret empty for local development: the verifier then short-circuits
# and `captchaProvider`/`captchaToken` may be omitted entirely.
CAPTCHA_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
CAPTCHA_TURNSTILE_SECRET=
CAPTCHA_RECAPTCHA_V2_SITE_KEY=
CAPTCHA_RECAPTCHA_V2_SECRET=
CAPTCHA_RECAPTCHA_V3_SITE_KEY=
CAPTCHA_RECAPTCHA_V3_SECRET=
CAPTCHA_HCAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
CAPTCHA_HCAPTCHA_SECRET=
# Extra hostnames whose provider-issued tokens are accepted, comma-separated.
# The ADMIN_APP_URL hostname is always allowed and does not need listing.
CAPTCHA_ALLOWED_HOSTNAMES=
CAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# reCAPTCHA v3 only — the score below which a token is treated as a bot.
CAPTCHA_RECAPTCHA_V3_MIN_SCORE=0.5

# ─── Mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `console` prints one-time codes to the log. That is exactly what makes it
# useful in development and unacceptable anywhere else — config.ts refuses it
# when NODE_ENV=production.
MAIL_TRANSPORT=console
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_FROM=admin-no-reply@orebit.io

# A second relay, tried when the primary will not take a message.
#
# Leave SMTP_BACKUP_HOST empty to disable failover entirely — that is the default.
# Setting it only has an effect when MAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp.
#
# Worth configuring in production: every admin sign-in requires an emailed code and
# there is no password-recovery flow behind it, so a single relay outage locks every
# admin out of the service with no other way in. Point this at a different provider
# from the primary, not a second host of the same one — a provider-wide outage or a
# suspended account takes both otherwise.
#
# Only an unreachable relay fails over. A rejected recipient does not: a 550 for a
# mailbox that does not exist will be a 550 here too.
SMTP_BACKUP_HOST=
SMTP_BACKUP_PORT=587
SMTP_BACKUP_USER=
SMTP_BACKUP_PASSWORD=

# ─── Seeding ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read by `npm run db:seed` to create the first SUPER_ADMIN. Admins are seeded,
# never self-registered — there is no signup endpoint. The seeded admin has NO
# second factor, so its first login goes through forced 2FA enrollment.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
SEED_ADMIN_NAME=

# ─── Reverse proxy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The proxies whose `X-Forwarded-For` this service will believe, as a
# comma-separated list of CIDRs or IPs. Empty means trust nobody — the socket
# address is the client, which is correct with no proxy in front.
#
# A LIST, not a boolean, and that is the whole point. Every rate limit and the
# entire captcha penalty ladder are keyed on the client IP. A blanket "trust the
# header" makes that IP a value the client writes: `proxy-addr` returns the
# leftmost XFF entry when every hop is trusted, and Cloudflare APPENDS to XFF
# rather than replacing it, so a caller who changes the header each request is
# never in the same bucket twice. Naming the proxies means the header is believed
# only for the hops that actually exist.
#
# Behind Cloudflare, list its published ranges (https://cloudflare.com/ips) or,
# better, the single address of the ingress in front of this process. Behind one
# reverse proxy on the same host: 127.0.0.1.
#
# Leave EMPTY unless a proxy really is in front. Getting this wrong in the
# permissive direction is the failure that silently disables every limit.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# ─── API documentation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Defaults to on everywhere except production, where opting in is deliberate —
# a full description of an admin auth API is a map of its doors.
DOCS_ENABLED=true

# ─── Object storage — Cloudflare R2 ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Where country flag images live. R2 speaks the S3 API, so these are ordinary S3
# coordinates.
#
# All five may be left blank in development: uploads then report
# `country_image_storage_unavailable` and every other surface works normally.
# Setting SOME of them is refused at boot — a half-configured bucket fails at the
# first upload instead, which is later and harder to diagnose. Production refuses
# to boot without all of them.
#
# ── R2_ENDPOINT vs R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL: these are NOT the same host ──────────
#
# R2_ENDPOINT is the authenticated S3 API. Every request to it is signed, and a
# browser cannot load an image from it. It is never returned to a client.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is what actually serves the bucket to the public — the
# r2.dev address, or better, a custom domain in front of it. Stored object KEYS
# are joined onto this at read time to produce the URLs the API returns, which is
# what makes changing CDN or domain a config change rather than a data migration.
#
# Pointing R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL at the S3 endpoint is the most common way to get
# this wrong: every image URL would 401.
#
# https only, and no query string or fragment — the public URL carries a `?v=`
# cache-buster appended by the service, and a base with its own query would break it.
R2_ENDPOINT=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ─── Session clocks — LOCAL TESTING ONLY ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The committed policy lives in auth/auth.constants.ts: a 30-minute inactivity
# window, a 5-minute activity write interval, a 15-minute access token. These
# three override it so the inactivity logout can actually be watched against a
# running frontend instead of waited out.
#
# Setting ANY of them with NODE_ENV=production is a boot failure, by design.
#
# Change them TOGETHER. The idle window alone is not enough: `touch` only writes
# `last_active_at` when the row is older than the write interval, so an interval
# above the window means activity is never recorded and an actively-working admin
# is logged out anyway — a test that looks like it passes while proving nothing.
# The rules (asserted at boot): access TTL < idle window < 12h ceiling, and the
# write interval at most half the idle window.
#
# A worked example — a 2-minute window keeping the production proportions:
#   SESSION_IDLE_SECONDS=120
#   SESSION_ACTIVITY_WRITE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=20
#   SESSION_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=60
#
# `expires_at` is stamped into the session row at sign-in, so existing sessions
# keep the old deadline: restart the service AND sign in again after changing these.
#SESSION_IDLE_SECONDS=
#SESSION_ACTIVITY_WRITE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=
#SESSION_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=
